{% set local_problem %}
<p>Surface downspout extensions work fine on flat lots with good drainage. They don't work on lakefront property.</p>

<p>The lots at <a href="/recent-projects/">Goose Pond</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">Lake Guntersville Shores</a>, and along <a href="/recent-projects/">Tupelo Pike</a> are sloped. That's intentional — the slope creates the views people pay a premium for. But "sloped toward the lake" often means "sloped past your foundation."</p>

<p>Water follows gravity. An extension that dumps water 6 feet from your foundation on a sloped lot doesn't solve the problem — the water just runs 6 feet sideways and continues downhill toward the lake. If your house is between the roof runoff and the lake, the water goes past your house. Past your basement wall. Past your lower-level living space.</p>

<p>We did a diagnostic at a house on Tupelo Pike where the homeowner had tried everything: longer extensions, splash blocks, a French drain along the foundation. Water still got in the basement every heavy storm. The issue was lot grade — 6% slope toward the lake. Every surface solution just delayed water reaching the foundation by a few feet. Underground drains that routed water 45 feet past the house to daylight at the lot edge solved it permanently.</p>

<p>The clay soil common in Scottsboro makes surface drainage even less effective. Clay doesn't absorb water — it sheds it. Water deposited on clay soil runs across the surface until it finds a low point. If that low point is your foundation, that's where it goes.</p>

<p>Underground drains bypass all of this. Water enters at the downspout, travels through a sealed pipe, and exits far enough from the house that lot grade works for you instead of against you.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water to a discharge point away from your foundation. Simple concept, but execution matters.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch corrugated or schedule 40 PVC depending on the application. Corrugated is cheaper and easier to route around obstacles. PVC is more durable and flows better — our preference for permanent installations. The pipe runs through a trench 8-12 inches deep, sloped at 1-2% grade so water flows by gravity.</p>

<p>At the discharge end, we have options: pop-up emitters that release water to the surface, daylight exits that open at grade level, or connections to municipal storm systems where available. For <a href="/recent-projects/">lake properties</a>, we often terminate the system near the water's edge where the grade naturally takes the water the rest of the way.</p>

<p>Key details that matter for longevity: cleanout access at direction changes (lets you clear clogs if they ever develop), proper slope throughout (flat or reverse sections hold water and eventually cause problems), and sized transition fittings from downspout to underground pipe (a 3x4 downspout shouldn't squeeze into a 3-inch adapter).</p>

<p>What we don't do: corrugated pipe with perforations (meant for French drains, not gutter discharge — they clog with silt), connections to septic systems (illegal and will cause failures), or undersized pipe (3-inch pipe clogs more easily than 4-inch and handles less volume).</p>
{% endset %}

